  <main id="main-content" class="seo-page"><div class="seo-answer"><strong>Direct answer:</strong> Bipolar mood disorders at Mind Grace Neuropsychiatric Clinic are managed with mood-stabilising medication, regular follow-up and psychoeducation so patients can recognise early warning signs of episodes and maintain daily functioning.</div>
<section class="seo-hero"><p class="seo-kicker">Mood concerns</p><h1>A careful assessment for changing mood</h1><p class="seo-lead">Mood shifts, periods of low mood, increased energy, reduced sleep, impulsivity, or irritability deserve careful context rather than quick conclusions.</p><div class="seo-actions"><a class="btn" href="/book">Ask about an assessment</a><a class="btn btn-outline" href="/psychiatry">Psychiatry consultations</a></div></section><section class="seo-section"><h2>Questions a clinician may consider</h2><div class="seo-grid"><article class="seo-card"><h3>Timeline</h3><p>Changes in sleep, energy, activity, mood, concentration, and functioning over time.</p></article><article class="seo-card"><h3>Context</h3><p>Health, medicines, substances, stressors, family history, and previous episodes may be relevant.</p></article><article class="seo-card"><h3>Plan</h3><p>Care may include follow-up, medication discussion, therapy, practical support, or referral.</p></article></div><div class="seo-note"><strong>Safety:</strong> If someone is at immediate risk, call 112 or attend the nearest emergency department.</div></section>    
  </main>
